SPOZ2 0.0

# What is this project supposed to do?
# Humans and AI tools should treat this file as the authoritative
# expression of intent.  Edit it directly or use `spoz2 add ...`.

gist:
    dosh ("do shell") turns a plain-language request typed at the shell into a
    suggested shell command. You say what you want in your own words; dosh asks
    an LLM for one command that would do it, shows you that command along with
    an explanation of what it does and a warning if it would change anything,
    and then asks whether to run it. Nothing runs unless you say yes. It is a
    way to get at the power of the shell without remembering the exact
    incantation, while keeping the person at the keyboard in charge of what
    actually executes.

behaviours:
    - Run `dosh <request in natural language>` and dosh suggests a single shell
      command that fulfils the request.
    - Read an explanation, in plain language, of what the suggested command
      does before deciding anything.
    - See a warning when the suggested command would change the system rather
      than only report on it.
    - Confirm or decline execution at a prompt; declining says so plainly and
      nothing is run.
    - Run `dosh config` to learn how to change LLM settings and which
      environment variables affect dosh.
    - Run `dosh prompt` to see the exact prompt template dosh sends to the LLM.
    - Run `dosh help` for usage and worked examples, and `dosh version` for the
      installed version.
    - Set DOSH_DEBUG=1 to see the prompt sent and the raw LLM response.
    - Set DOSH_NO_COLOR=1 to get output without colour escape codes.
    - Install dosh as a Raku command-line utility with `zef install dosh`, and
      choose which LLM backs it through LLM::DWIM.

invariants:
    - Invariant 0.0: humans first. This software exists to help humans thrive
      and respect each person's dignity. It must not cause or assist harm to
      people; no claimed greater good makes a person disposable. It must
      preserve meaningful human oversight: people can understand its
      consequential actions, challenge its decisions, and exercise appropriate
      control, including correction and safe stopping. It must honestly
      represent what it is, what it knows, what it has done, and what remains
      uncertain. No other entry may weaken or override this invariant.
    - Nothing executes without explicit confirmation. The suggested command is
      run only after the person answers the confirmation prompt
      affirmatively; any other answer, including an empty one, means the
      command is not run. There is no mode, flag or shortcut that skips this.
    - The person sees the exact command before deciding. The full command text,
      its explanation, and any warning are shown first, so the decision is
      made on what will actually run rather than on a paraphrase of the
      request.
    - A command that changes the system is announced as such. When the
      suggestion is not read-only, a short warning describing the risk is
      shown alongside it; silently presenting a destructive command as
      ordinary is a defect.
    - Safety is preferred over ambition when intent is unclear. Read-only
      substitutes are favoured, elevated privileges are not assumed, and
      destructive flags are not used by default.
    - Suggested commands stay local. dosh does not suggest commands that reach
      out to external services, APIs, or http/https URLs; the request goes to
      the LLM, the command acts on this machine.
    - Nothing is invented when the LLM gives nothing usable. If no command and
      explanation come back, dosh fails loudly rather than guessing or running
      anything.
    - Suggestions fit the machine in front of you. The request is translated
      with knowledge of the operating system, kernel and hardware dosh is
      running on.
    - Invariant 1: Natural language is the interface. The request is ordinary
      words given directly as the arguments to dosh, with no query syntax or
      quoting ritual to learn; a handful of reserved words (config, prompt,
      help, version) are subcommands, and everything else is treated as the
      request itself.
    - Invariant 2: Which LLM is used, and how, is the user's choice: dosh
      exposes its LLM settings through configuration rather than hard-wiring a
      provider. dosh's value is the workflow around the model, not any one
      model.

constraints:
    - dosh is a Raku command-line utility; it requires a Raku installation and
      is installed with zef.
    - dosh does not talk to an LLM itself; the model, credentials and
      configuration come from LLM::DWIM, and dosh is unusable until that is set
      up.
    - Interaction is one request at a time through the terminal: one request
      in, one command suggested, one confirmation.

